- `ui-tests`: `OpticalConstructor.Ui.Tests.KitStartWindowTests`

**How to implement.** A new kit's identity is injected, as an item's is, so a kit built twice by the same messages is the same kit file (001 §Q 'It asserts that the built kit equals the committed kit, file by file'; AGENTS.md 'Inject ambient effects (time, randomness) as provider records'; .spec-md §4 'Errors and IO'). Today the one mint is `KitId (Guid.NewGuid ())` inside `KitOpening.openKitAt`'s read-write path (step 150), and no start-screen seam reaches it (steps 176, 177). Each doc is one to three lines citing spec 0050 S12.1. (1) `OpticalConstructor.Domain/Kit.fs`, directly after `ItemIdProvider` (step 236): `[<ReferenceEquality>] KitIdProvider = { mintKitId : unit -> KitId }`, the provider that mints a new kit's identity (step 24's `KitId`). (2) `OpticalConstructor.Ui/KitOpening.fs`, beside `ItemIdProvider.system` (step 239): `type KitIdProvider with static member system : KitIdProvider` mints `KitId (Guid.NewGuid ())` at each call. `openKitAt`'s body moves into `openKitWith (kitIds : KitIdProvider) (folder : KitFolder) (access : KitAccess) (clock : ClockProvider) (author : KitAuthor) (imports : CatalogueImportContext) : Result<OpenedKit, KitLibraryError>`, whose read-write path initialises an empty folder with `folder.namedKitFile (kitIds.mintKitId ()) author` (step 145) and calls `mintKitId` only when it initialises; `openKitAt` keeps its signature and is `openKitWith KitIdProvider.system`, so none of its callers changes. A read-only open never initialises and never calls the provider (step 150). (3) `OpticalConstructor.Ui/KitStartView.fs`: `KitStartContext` (steps 176, 190, 230) gains `kitIds : KitIdProvider` directly after `clock`, and `CreateKit` (step 177) opens through `openKitWith m.context.kitIds` where it called `openKitAt`; `OpenKit`, `OpenKitReadOnly` and `OpenKitZip` are unchanged. `OpticalConstructor.Ui/KitStartWindow.fs` (steps 179, 230, 232) composes `kitIds = KitIdProvider.system`. Every other literal of `KitStartContext` (`KitStartTests.fs`; `KitStartWindowTests.fs` where it builds one) composes `KitIdProvider.system`, its assertions unchanged. No other field, message or option is added; `scratchKit` (step 150) keeps its mint, because a scratch kit is never written. (4) Tests, in `OpticalConstructor.Tests/KitStartTests.fs`: `a new kit takes its id from the start screen's kit-id provider`: over a context whose `kitIds` answers `KitId (Guid.Parse "e63c9d6e-7961-4d9f-9c8d-e5a151414e0e")`, `CreateKit` of a fresh folder under the test output hands over a kit whose kit file holds that id, and `(KitProxy.create folder).readKitFile ()` reads the same id from `.kit-json`; `opening an existing kit, read-write or read-only, mints no kit id`: over a context whose `kitIds` counts its calls, `OpenKit` and `OpenKitReadOnly` of that kit (the second after the first is closed) hand it over with its stored id and the count stays 0.

**Acceptance.** After the provider lands, `CreateKit` MUST initialise a kit whose id the start screen's `KitIdProvider` mints, `openKitAt` MUST keep its signature over `KitIdProvider.system`, and opening an existing kit MUST mint no id; constructor-unit-tests MUST pass, including `a new kit takes its id from the start screen's kit-id provider` and `opening an existing kit, read-write or read-only, mints no kit id`, the count rising by 2; ui-smoke MUST pass; ui-tests MUST pass over the selected class, its count unchanged; and build, elevation and the twenty-one s41 invariants MUST pass.
